I Serving | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 Validation campaign (Investigation)\"] --> E002[\"E002 vLLM ROCm runtime productization\"]\n    E001 --> E003[\"E003 KServe serving-stack enablement\"]\n    E001 --> E006[\"E006 Documentation\"]\n    E002 --> E006\n    E003 --> E006\n    E004[\"E004 PyTorch ROCm training\"] --> E006\n    E005[\"E005 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota\"] --> E006\n```\n\nCritical path: E001 → E002 → E006 (length 3).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge (or absence) | Rule | Rationale |\n|-------------------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The campaign outcome (go/no-go and the cleared model list) determines the scope of the productized runtime — a true gate. `rewrite`: if only a subset of models clears, productize only those; a full no-go removes the productization scope. |\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The serving-stack enablement scope follows the same go/no-go and cleared model list. `rewrite` for a partial clear, scope removal on a full no-go. |\n| E001 → E006 | Rule 11 + content input | The documentation must state the validated model list, which is the campaign's deliverable. |\n| E002 → E006, E003 → E006, E004 → E006, E005 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy — docs describe what was built. |\n| No edge E002 ↔ E003 | Rule 13 (configuration reference) | The productized runtime image consumed by the serving-stack runtime registration is a configuration reference (image digest), not a build-against artifact — the coupling is an AC gate on E003 (\"references the pinned productized ROCm runtime image\"), never a DAG edge. |\n| No edge E004 ↔ E005 | Rules 14/15 | Training Operator and Kueue live in different repos with no shared build-against artifact; the end-to-end admission flow is validated via acceptance criteria on both epics rather than a serializing edge. |\n| No edges serving ↔ training (E001/E002/E003 ↔ E004/E005) | Rule 14 | Serving and training enablement are independent work streams in different repos; the training side does not build on the serving campaign's outcome. |\n\nNo `konflux-onboarding`, `repo-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics are\ngenerated: all four affected components are in the platform (architecture\ncontext files exist) and Konflux-onboarded per those files, so the onboarding\nchain (Rule 20) does not apply. The ROCm runtime build flows through the\nexisting Serving Runtimes pipeline; license scanning of the new ROCm/AMD\ntransitive dependencies is an acceptance criterion on E002.\n\nThe campaign's bounded outcomes (all models clear / a subset clears / no-go)\nvary the scope of the same productization and serving-stack epics rather than\nwhich epics exist, so no conditional branch files are emitted — the outcome is\ncarried by `gated_by` / `gate_failure_impact` on E002 and E003.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic | Notes |\n|-----|----------|-------------|------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware establishing functional and performance parity for vLLM serving, producing the go/no-go scope | RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | Fully covered; the campaign is the Investigation gating the P0 productization. |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | Supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime productized for cleared models | RHAISTRAT-9020-E002, RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | Split along the component boundary (Serving Runtimes builds the runtime; KServe exposes and serves it end to end). |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | PyTorch training on ROCm for single- and multi-GPU jobs admitted through Kueue, with amd.com/gpu quota handling | RHAISTRAT-9020-E004, RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Split along the component boundary (Training Operator runs the jobs; Distributed Workloads handles Kueue quota). |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation of supported AMD hardware, driver prerequisites, and the validated model list | RHAISTRAT-9020-E006 | Separate docs-authoring epic; its priority is P1 via the docs-authoring exception (strategy frontmatter priority `Major` → P1), not via inheritance from the P2 HLR. |\n\nEvery HLR is covered; no HLR is orphaned and no lower-priority HLR is folded\ninto a higher-priority epic.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n  Dependencies table, no Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then), and no\n  Non-Functional Requirements section. Teams were inferred from the\n  architecture context owning teams; acceptance criteria were derived from the\n  HLRs at the functional level. Non-blocking, but the refinement gap is real.\n- **Hardware access (open question 1) is an availability precondition, not an\n  epic gate**: procurement or partner-lab access to dedicated AMD Instinct\n  nodes is externally controlled. It does not change which epics exist or what\n  they do — the campaign epic exists either way and securing access is part of\n  its scope — so it is recorded as a risk on E001 (and noted on E004/E005),\n  not as an acceptance criterion or a separate Investigation.\n- **Upstream ROCm build cadence (open question 2) is an externally controlled\n  timing risk**: whether AMD's runtime image releases align with our release\n  trains does not change the epic structure — the productization epic tracks\n  and pins upstream releases either way. Recorded as a risk plus a pinning /\n  release-alignment acceptance criterion on E002.\n- **KServe scope assumption**: the strategy lists KServe as an affected\n  component without stating what changes. E003 is scoped at the functional\n  level (runtime exposure, AMD accelerator resource handling, end-to-end\n  serving validation); implementation decisions are left to the team.\n- **Documentation team assumption**: no owning team is stated for the\n  Documentation component; E006 is assigned to RHOAI Serving as the team\n  owning the P0 serving scope, with technical review expected from the\n  Distributed Workloads team for the training content.\n- **Docs priority exception (not an inversion)**: HLR-4 is P2 while E006 is\n  P1 — this follows the docs-authoring priority rule (strategy priority\n  `Major` → P1), which exempts docs epics from HLR priority inheritance.\n- **Planning note (epic size)**: the validation campaign epic (E001) is\n  potentially above the ~2-week guideline — benchmarking the full supported\n  model set on physical hardware, plus hardware-access logistics, can exceed\n  one sprint. Kept as one logical change: a split would fragment a single\n  campaign and duplicate its methodology and hardware setup. **Re-split\n  trigger**: re-split this epic by model family if the campaign outgrows a\n  sprint of effort or the supported model set expands beyond the current\n  estimate.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nThe externally timed dependency (AMD/upstream ROCm image cadence) and the\ninternal gate (campaign go/no-go) justify a two-tier plan:\n\n- **Tier 1 (independent value, starts immediately)**: E001 (risk-reduction\n  validation campaign — first-hand parity data has value even if\n  productization is deferred), E004 and E005 (ROCm training support delivers\n  user-facing value independent of the serving campaign's outcome).\n- **Tier 2 (gated on the Tier 1 campaign outcome)**: E002 and E003\n  (productization and serving-stack enablement proceed only for the models the\n  campaign clears).\n- **Always last**: E006 (documentation reflects what was actually built and\n  validated).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":48012,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab044e5d9d10986e","agent_id":"a11b7706a09d57009","session_id":"da64fd2d-69d4-4f10-98cf-a5e53b041b70","uuid":"40a0fc8b-fb43-4fd0-9362-d81e006be3bd","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:29:30.905Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9020