{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-9643e65b-49fd-417f-a66b-d8944ed495b1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-977ced1543c1a590","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9020-pgpp1i0v/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9020-E001.md","content":"# AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM ROCm serving\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the first-hand functional and performance parity data for\nvLLM serving on AMD Instinct accelerators (ROCm) that the platform currently\nlacks — today only CUDA (NVIDIA) runtime builds are productized and no\nfirst-hand AMD data exists for the supported model set. It runs a validation\ncampaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware across the supported model set and\nproduces the go/no-go scope — the list of models cleared for productization —\nthat the serving productization work is gated on.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Define the campaign methodology: hardware configuration, ROCm and driver\n  versions, model versions, and serving workload definitions, recorded so the\n  results are reproducible.\n- Execute functional validation: serve each model in the supported model set\n  with vLLM on ROCm and record a pass/fail outcome per model.\n- Execute performance validation: capture serving performance metrics for the\n  same model set against the CUDA baseline builds.\n- Produce the go/no-go recommendation: the parity report and the validated\n  (cleared) model list that productization will build against.\n- Coordinate access to dedicated AMD Instinct nodes (procurement or\n  partner-lab access) as part of the campaign logistics — see Risks.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Functional parity results are recorded for every model in the supported\n  model set, with a pass/fail outcome per model, from vLLM serving runs on\n  physical AMD Instinct hardware.\n- Performance parity data for the same model set is captured against the CUDA\n  baseline, with the comparison metrics documented.\n- The go/no-go recommendation with the cleared model list is published as the\n  campaign report.\n- The campaign methodology (hardware, driver/ROCm versions, model versions,\n  workloads) is documented so the results are reproducible.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): a validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware\n  establishing functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the\n  supported model set, producing the go/no-go scope for productization —\n  fully covered by this epic.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- Hardware access is an availability precondition controlled outside the\n  strategy (procurement or partner-lab negotiation); it is tracked here as a\n  risk rather than an acceptance criterion. The epic's own deliverable is the\n  validated campaign report.\n- Upstream ROCm image cadence is controlled by AMD and the upstream\n  communities; the campaign should pin and record the ROCm versions used so\n  the results are attributable.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":50748,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab044e5d9d10986e","agent_id":"a11b7706a09d57009","session_id":"da64fd2d-69d4-4f10-98cf-a5e53b041b70","uuid":"7f0cc473-676b-4958-86ee-a27f3f7cfd99","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:29:42.655Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9020"}
