{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-873d7b4786690cf4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020 (AMD GPU / ROCm enablement for serving and training)\n11\t\n12\tThe strategy was not triaged in Step 0: it spans four components and two owning\n13\tteams, mixes P0/P1/P2 requirements, and its P0 core (the validation campaign)\n14\tis a genuine unknown gating the productization work. It is not documentation-\n15\tonly: the Serving Runtimes, KServe, Training Operator, and Distributed\n16\tWorkloads components all require code or configuration changes.\n17\t\n18\tTeam assignments are inferred from the architecture context files\n19\t(`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/`), because the strategy\n20\thas no Impacted Teams table: KServe and Serving Runtimes are owned by RHOAI\n21\tServing; Training Operator and Distributed Workloads are owned by the\n22\tDistributed Workloads team.\n23\t\n24\t## Epic List\n25\t\n26\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n27\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM ROCm serving | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize the vLLM ROCm serving runtime for validated models | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | KServe serving-stack enablement for AMD GPU (ROCm) serving | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | PyTorch ROCm training support via the Training Operator | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Kueue quota handling for amd.com/gpu training workloads | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E006 | Document AMD GPU (ROCm) support | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n34\t\n35\t## Dependency DAG\n36\t\n37\t```mermaid\n38\tgraph TD\n39\t    E001[\"E001 Validation campaign (Investigation)\"] --> E002[\"E002 vLLM ROCm runtime productization\"]\n40\t    E001 --> E003[\"E003 KServe serving-stack enablement\"]\n41\t    E001 --> E006[\"E006 Documentation\"]\n42\t    E002 --> E006\n43\t    E003 --> E006\n44\t    E004[\"E004 PyTorch ROCm training\"] --> E006\n45\t    E005[\"E005 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota\"] --> E006\n46\t```\n47\t\n48\tCritical path: E001 → E002 → E006 (length 3).\n49\t\n50\t## DAG Justification\n51\t\n52\t| Edge (or absence) | Rule | Rationale |\n53\t|-------------------|------|-----------|\n54\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The campaign outcome (go/no-go and the cleared model list) determines the scope of the productized runtime — a true gate. `rewrite`: if only a subset of models clears, productize only those; a full no-go removes the productization scope. |\n55\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The serving-stack enablement scope follows the same go/no-go and cleared model list. `rewrite` for a partial clear, scope removal on a full no-go. |\n56\t| E001 → E006 | Rule 11 + content input | The documentation must state the validated model list, which is the campaign's deliverable. |\n57\t| E002 → E006, E003 → E006, E004 → E006, E005 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy — docs describe what was built. |\n58\t| No edge E002 ↔ E003 | Rule 13 (configuration reference) | The productized runtime image consumed by the serving-stack runtime registration is a configuration reference (image digest), not a build-against artifact — the coupling is an AC gate on E003 (\"references the pinned productized ROCm runtime image\"), never a DAG edge. |\n59\t| No edge E004 ↔ E005 | Rules 14/15 | Training Operator and Kueue live in different repos with no shared build-against artifact; the end-to-end admission flow is validated via acceptance criteria on both epics rather than a serializing edge. |\n60\t| No edges serving ↔ training (E001/E002/E003 ↔ E004/E005) | Rule 14 | Serving and training enablement are independent work streams in different repos; the training side does not build on the serving campaign's outcome. |\n61\t\n62\tNo `konflux-onboarding`, `repo-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics are\n63\tgenerated: all four affected components are in the platform (architecture\n64\tcontext files exist) and Konflux-onboarded per those files, so the onboarding\n65\tchain (Rule 20) does not apply. The ROCm runtime build flows through the\n66\texisting Serving Runtimes pipeline; license scanning of the new ROCm/AMD\n67\ttransitive dependencies is an acceptance criterion on E002.\n68\t\n69\tThe campaign's bounded outcomes (all models clear / a subset clears / no-go)\n70\tvary the scope of the same productization and serving-stack epics rather than\n71\twhich epics exist, so no conditional branch files are emitted — the outcome is\n72\tcarried by `gated_by` / `gate_failure_impact` on E002 and E003.\n73\t\n74\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n75\t\n76\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic | Notes |\n77\t|-----|----------|-------------|------|-------|\n78\t| HLR-1 | P0 | Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware establishing functional and performance parity for vLLM serving, producing the go/no-go scope | RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | Fully covered; the campaign is the Investigation gating the P0 productization. |\n79\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime productized for cleared models | RHAISTRAT-9020-E002, RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | Split along the component boundary (Serving Runtimes builds the runtime; KServe exposes and serves it end to end). |\n80\t| HLR-3 | P1 | PyTorch training on ROCm for single- and multi-GPU jobs admitted through Kueue, with amd.com/gpu quota handling | RHAISTRAT-9020-E004, RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Split along the component boundary (Training Operator runs the jobs; Distributed Workloads handles Kueue quota). |\n81\t| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation of supported AMD hardware, driver prerequisites, and the validated model list | RHAISTRAT-9020-E006 | Separate docs-authoring epic; its priority is P1 via the docs-authoring exception (strategy frontmatter priority `Major` → P1), not via inheritance from the P2 HLR. |\n82\t\n83\tEvery HLR is covered; no HLR is orphaned and no lower-priority HLR is folded\n84\tinto a higher-priority epic.\n85\t\n86\t## Health Warnings\n87\t\n88\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n89\t  Dependencies table, no Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then), and no\n90\t  Non-Functional Requirements section. Teams were inferred from the\n91\t  architecture context owning teams; acceptance criteria were derived from the\n92\t  HLRs at the functional level. Non-blocking, but the refinement gap is real.\n93\t- **Hardware access (open question 1) is an availability precondition, not an\n94\t  epic gate**: procurement or partner-lab access to dedicated AMD Instinct\n95\t  nodes is externally controlled. It does not change which epics exist or what\n96\t  they do — the campaign epic exists either way and securing access is part of\n97\t  its scope — so it is recorded as a risk on E001 (and noted on E004/E005),\n98\t  not as an acceptance criterion or a separate Investigation.\n99\t- **Upstream ROCm build cadence (open question 2) is an externally controlled\n100\t  timing risk**: whether AMD's runtime image releases align with our release\n101\t  trains does not change the epic structure — the productization epic tracks\n102\t  and pins upstream releases either way. Recorded as a risk plus a pinning /\n103\t  release-alignment acceptance criterion on E002.\n104\t- **KServe scope assumption**: the strategy lists KServe as an affected\n105\t  component without stating what changes. E003 is scoped at the functional\n106\t  level (runtime exposure, AMD accelerator resource handling, end-to-end\n107\t  serving validation); implementation decisions are left to the team.\n108\t- **Documentation team assumption**: no owning team is stated for the\n109\t  Documentation component; E006 is assigned to RHOAI Serving as the team\n110\t  owning the P0 serving scope, with technical review expected from the\n111\t  Distributed Workloads team for the training content.\n112\t- **Docs priority exception (not an inversion)**: HLR-4 is P2 while E006 is\n113\t  P1 — this follows the docs-authoring priority rule (strategy priority\n114\t  `Major` → P1), which exempts docs epics from HLR priority inheritance.\n115\t- **Planning note (epic size)**: the validation campaign epic (E001) is\n116\t  potentially above the ~2-week guideline — benchmarking the full supported\n117\t  model set on physical hardware, plus hardware-access logistics, can exceed\n118\t  one sprint. Kept as one logical change: a split would fragment a single\n119\t  campaign and duplicate its methodology and hardware setup. **Re-split\n120\t  trigger**: re-split this epic by model family if the campaign outgrows a\n121\t  sprint of effort or the supported model set expands beyond the current\n122\t  estimate.\n123\t\n124\t## Tiered Delivery\n125\t\n126\tThe externally timed dependency (AMD/upstream ROCm image cadence) and the\n127\tinternal gate (campaign go/no-go) justify a two-tier plan:\n128\t\n129\t- **Tier 1 (independent value, starts immediately)**: E001 (risk-reduction\n130\t  validation campaign — first-hand parity data has value even if\n131\t  productization is deferred), E004 and E005 (ROCm training support delivers\n132\t  user-facing value independent of the serving campaign's outcome).\n133\t- **Tier 2 (gated on the Tier 1 campaign outcome)**: E002 and E003\n134\t  (productization and serving-stack enablement proceed only for the models the\n135\t  campaign clears).\n136\t- **Always last**: E006 (documentation reflects what was actually built and\n137\t  validated).\n138\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b885dddbb438732c","agent_id":"a83ade6d1ea306758","session_id":"da64fd2d-69d4-4f10-98cf-a5e53b041b70","uuid":"79e9043f-e3fe-4561-a1bb-ce5e255e73c2","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:32:29.432Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-873d7b4786690cf4","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
