AG matches frontmatter\n39\texactly and correctly uses AC gates (not edges) for the image-digest and\n40\tadmission-flow couplings, epic boundaries respect team/component tuples with\n41\ta properly recorded re-split trigger on the campaign epic, signal scoring is\n42\twell calibrated against the architecture-context files, and strategy-level\n43\trisks are carried forward rather than dropped. It fails on one structural\n44\tpoint: the docs epic lists the validation-campaign Investigation in its\n45\t`dependencies` without `gated_by`, which is a gating-metadata defect that\n46\tbreaks automated gate detection (criterion 4 scores 0, forcing a fail under\n47\tthe auto-fail rule despite the 12/14 total).\n48\t\n49\t## Criterion Details\n50\t\n51\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n52\t\n53\tAll four HLRs map to epics: HLR-1 (P0) → E001; HLR-2 (P0) → E002 + E003,\n54\tsplit along the Serving Runtimes / KServe component boundary; HLR-3 (P1) →\n55\tE004 + E005, split along the Training Operator / Distributed Workloads\n56\tboundary; HLR-4 (P2) → E006. The traceability matrix is present in the\n57\tsummary and every epic carries an HLR Traceability section.\n58\t\n59\tPriority inheritance is correct: E001 (P0) blocks the P0 epics E002/E003 and\n60\tis itself P0. E006 is P1 while blocking nothing (leaf node) and is covered by\n61\tthe docs-authoring exception (strategy priority `Major` → P1), which the\n62\tsummary explicitly documents — not an inversion. No priority collapse: no\n63\tepic maps to HLRs at more than one priority level, so no deferrable\n64\tlower-priority feature is folded into a higher-priority epic.\n65\t\n66\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n67\t\n68\tDiagram/frontmatter cross-check: all seven edges in the mermaid diagram\n69\t(E001→E002, E001→E003, E001→E006, E002→E006, E003→E006, E004→E006,\n70\tE005→E006) have matching `dependencies` entries in the target epics'\n71\tfrontmatter, and vice versa; arrows run dependency → dependent. No circular\n72\tdependencies, no edges to nonexistent epics.\n73\t\n74\tEdge justification is rule-compliant and notably careful on the parallelism\n75\ttraps: no E002↔E003 edge — the productized runtime image consumed by E003 is\n76\ta configuration reference (image digest) correctly expressed as an AC gate on\n77\tE003's first acceptance criterion, per Rule 13; no E004↔E005 edge (different\n78\trepos, no shared build-against artifact), with the admission-flow coupling\n79\tcarried by ACs on both epics; no serving↔training edges (independent work\n80\tstreams). Rule 11 is satisfied: the docs-authoring epic E006 is blocked by\n81\tevery Implementation epic. No konflux-onboarding / repo-onboarding /\n82\tlicense-validation epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 parallel-edge checks do not\n83\tapply; the summary justifies omitting the onboarding chain (all four affected\n84\tcomponents have architecture-context files). Critical path length 3\n85\t(E001→E002→E006) is within the M-standard heuristic (3-4). No conditional\n86\tbranch files are emitted, with an explicit and sound rationale: the three\n87\tbounded campaign outcomes vary the scope of the same epics (handled by\n88\t`gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` on E002/E003) rather than which epics exist.\n89\t\n90\tCross-check of epic content for uncaptured data/artifact dependencies: E005's\n91\tadmission validation needs runnable ROCm PyTorchJobs, but the PyTorchJob CRD\n92\tand upstream ROCm images are existing foundations, not artifacts produced by\n93\tE004 — no missing edge. The E003→image coupling is an AC gate by rule (its\n94\tabsence from the DAG is the compliant shape, per Rule 13).\n95\t\n96\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n97\t\n98\tEach component/team tuple produces a separate epic: Serving Runtimes/RHOAI\n99\tServing (E001, E002), KServe/RHOAI Serving (E003), Training\n100\tOperator/Distributed Workloads (E004), Distributed Workloads/Distributed\n101\tWorkloads (E005), Documentation/RHOAI Serving (E006). No epic bundles work\n102\tacross teams.\n103\t\n104\tSize: E001 (the validation campaign) is acknowledged as potentially above the\n105\t~2-week guideline and kept as one logical change with a concrete recorded\n106\tre-split trigger (\"re-split by model family if the campaign outgrows a sprint\n107\tof effort or the supported model set expands\") in the summary's Health\n108\tWarnings — a managed trade-off that counts as within the guideline, not a\n109\tsize violation.\n110\t\n111\tScope duplication: E002 builds the runtime image, E003 exposes it in the\n112\tserving stack — different components and repos, no rebuilt surface. E004 and\n113\tE005 each validate the shared admission seam from their own side, which is\n114\tintegration-seam coordination, not independent construction of the same\n115\tcomponent/API. Work-product types are not mixed: E006 is pure content\n116\tauthoring; all other epics are code/config implementation.\n117\t\n118\t### 4. Type Correctness (0/2)\n119\t\n120\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (go/no-go and the cleared model\n121\tlist) determines the scope and existence of E002/E003 — exactly the\n122\tRule 3 true-gate case. E002 and E003 correctly carry `gated_by:\n123\tRHAISTRAT-9020-E001` (present in their direct `dependencies`) with\n124\t`gate_failure_impact` populated (action `rewrite`, fallback covering both the\n125\tpartial-clear rewrite and the full-no-go scope removal). E002-E006 are\n126\tImplementations producing concrete artifacts.\n127\t\n128\t**Major issue — missing gate metadata on E006:** E006 lists the\n129\tInvestigation E001 in its `dependencies` (a content-input edge for the\n130\tvalidated model list) but sets `gated_by: null` and `gate_failure_impact:\n131\tnull`. Per Rule 3 and the frontmatter contract, every epic that depends on an\n132\tInvestigation must carry `gated_by` pointing at it: the campaign outcome\n133\tchanges E006's scope (which models are documented as validated; a full no-go\n134\tremoves the serving content), so this is a true gate, not a mere scheduling\n135\tdependency. Automated consumers use `gated_by` to detect gates and will\n136\tsilently miss this one. Correction: set `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9020-E001` on\n137\tE006 with `gate_failure_impact` (action `rewrite`; fallback: documentation\n138\treflects only the cleared model list, with serving sections removed on a full\n139\tno-go) — or, alternatively, drop E001 from E006's `dependencies` and let the\n140\tmodel list reach the docs through the E002/E003 implementations that E006\n141\talready blocks on. As written, the major issue forces this criterion to 0.\n142\t\n143\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n144\t\n145\tType-appropriate signal fields are used throughout: E001 carries\n146\t`investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric); E002-E006 carry `ai_signals`\n147\t(9-signal rubric). No cross-type contamination.\n148\t\n149\tE001's investigation signals match the rubric: `question_specificity: +1`\n150\t(per-model pass/fail with a defined go/no-go criterion),\n151\t`source_accessibility: 0` (no readable artifact holds the parity answer — it\n152\tmust be produced by running the campaign), `local_runnability: 0` (requires\n153\tphysical AMD Instinct hardware), `cluster_hardware_dependence: -2` (the\n154\tgating question needs hardware absent from the CI fleet),\n155\t`human_judgment_required: -1` (peripheral procurement/sign-off items only).\n156\tRationales in the ai-signals file match the frontmatter values.\n157\t\n158\tImplementation signals are consistent and well grounded: E002's\n159\t`open_questions: -1` / `external_dependency: -1` are justified by the\n160\tcampaign-gated model scope and AMD-controlled image cadence;\n161\t`architecture_claims: +1` on E002/E003/E004/E005 each cites the correct\n162\t`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/` file\n163\t(serving-runtimes.md, kserve.md, training-operator.md,\n164\tdistributed-workloads.md) that corroborates the strategy's premise. E006's\n165\t`architecture_claims: 0` is correct — no architecture-context file covers the\n166\tDocumentation component, so there is nothing to corroborate. E006's\n167\t`human_process_gates: -1` (docs technical review) is justified. No arithmetic\n168\tor threshold checks were performed (pipeline-computed).\n169\t\n170\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n171\t\n172\tEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria derived from the HLRs.\n173\tRule-mandated ACs are present: E006 (docs-authoring) has the\n174\t\"technically reviewed against the implementations\" AC (Rule 24); E002\n175\tcarries a build/test/release pipeline-green AC (Rule 25) even though no\n176\tkonflux-onboarding epic exists; no epic replaces an existing capability (the\n177\tROCm variant is additive; CUDA builds remain), so Rule 23 rollback/flag ACs\n178\tdo not apply.\n179\t\n180\tSoft-AC scan is clean: the hardware-access and upstream-cadence\n181\texternally-controlled preconditions are correctly recorded as Risks/Notes\n182\t(E001, E002, E004, E005) and Health Warnings rather than misfiled as ACs.\n183\tE001's ACs are stated on its own deliverable (recorded results, published\n184\treport), and E002's \"serves each cleared model on AMD Instinct hardware\" AC\n185\tconditions what the epic itself verifies — valid. No epic body references\n186\tsibling epics by draft ID (prose says \"the runtime productization work\", not\n187\t\"E002\").\n188\t\n189\tOne minor nit: E004's scope bullet \"exercise job admission through the\n190\tplatform's queueing layer ... validated here from the job side\" has no\n191\tcorresponding acceptance criterion — E004's ACs cover job completion and\n192\ttraining correctness only, and the admission ACs live on E005. Add a job-side\n193\tadmission AC to E004 or trim the scope claim. Not score-reducing on its own.\n194\t\n195\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n196\t\n197\tThe combined epic set accounts for the full strategy scope: validation\n198\tcampaign (E001), runtime productization (E002), KServe enab