ion flow is validated via acceptance criteria on both epics rather than a serializing edge. |\n59\t| No edges serving ↔ training (E001/E002/E003 ↔ E004/E005) | Rule 14 | Serving and training enablement are independent work streams in different repos; the training side does not build on the serving campaign's outcome. |\n60\t\n61\tNo `konflux-onboarding`, `repo-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics are\n62\tgenerated: all four affected components are in the platform (architecture\n63\tcontext files exist) and Konflux-onboarded per those files, so the onboarding\n64\tchain (Rule 20) does not apply. The ROCm runtime build flows through the\n65\texisting Serving Runtimes pipeline; license scanning of the new ROCm/AMD\n66\ttransitive dependencies is an acceptance criterion on E002.\n67\t\n68\tThe campaign's bounded outcomes (all models clear / a subset clears / no-go)\n69\tvary the scope of the same productization and serving-stack epics rather than\n70\twhich epics exist, so no conditional branch files are emitted — the outcome is\n71\tcarried by `gated_by` / `gate_failure_impact` on E002 and E003.\n72\t\n73\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n74\t\n75\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic | Notes |\n76\t|-----|----------|-------------|------|-------|\n77\t| HLR-1 | P0 | Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware establishing functional and performance parity for vLLM serving, producing the go/no-go scope | RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | Fully covered; the campaign is the Investigation gating the P0 productization. |\n78\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime productized for cleared models | RHAISTRAT-9020-E002, RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | Split along the component boundary (Serving Runtimes builds the runtime; KServe exposes and serves it end to end). |\n79\t| HLR-3 | P1 | PyTorch training on ROCm for single- and multi-GPU jobs admitted through Kueue, with amd.com/gpu quota handling | RHAISTRAT-9020-E004, RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Split along the component boundary (Training Operator runs the jobs; Distributed Workloads handles Kueue quota). |\n80\t| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation of supported AMD hardware, driver prerequisites, and the validated model list | RHAISTRAT-9020-E006 | Separate docs-authoring epic; its priority is P1 via the docs-authoring exception (strategy frontmatter priority `Major` → P1), not via inheritance from the P2 HLR. |\n81\t\n82\tEvery HLR is covered; no HLR is orphaned and no lower-priority HLR is folded\n83\tinto a higher-priority epic.\n84\t\n85\t## Health Warnings\n86\t\n87\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n88\t  Dependencies table, no Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then), and no\n89\t  Non-Functional Requirements section. Teams were inferred from the\n90\t  architecture context owning teams; acceptance criteria were derived from the\n91\t  HLRs at the functional level. Non-blocking, but the refinement gap is real.\n92\t- **Hardware access (open question 1) is an availability precondition, not an\n93\t  epic gate**: procurement or partner-lab access to dedicated AMD Instinct\n94\t  nodes is externally controlled. It does not change which epics exist or what\n95\t  they do — the campaign epic exists either way and securing access is part of\n96\t  its scope — so it is recorded as a risk on E001 (and noted on E004/E005),\n97\t  not as an acceptance criterion or a separate Investigation.\n98\t- **Upstream ROCm build cadence (open question 2) is an externally controlled\n99\t  timing risk**: whether AMD's runtime image releases align with our release\n100\t  trains does not change the epic structure — the productization epic tracks\n101\t  and pins upstream releases either way. Recorded as a risk plus a pinning /\n102\t  release-alignment acceptance criterion on E002.\n103\t- **KServe scope assumption**: the strategy lists KServe as an affected\n104\t  component without stating what changes. E003 is scoped at the functional\n105\t  level (runtime exposure, A